# Distribute Elements Into Two Arrays I

## Problem Statement

Given an integer array `nums`, distribute its elements into two arrays `arr1` and `arr2`.

Initially:

- `arr1 = [nums[0]]`
- `arr2 = [nums[1]]`

For every remaining element `nums[i]`:

- If the last element of `arr1` is greater than the last element of `arr2`, append `nums[i]` to `arr1`.
- Otherwise, append `nums[i]` to `arr2`.

Finally, concatenate `arr1` and `arr2` and return the resulting array.

## Approach

We maintain two arrays and compare their last elements while processing the remaining elements.

At every step:

1. Compare `arr1.back()` and `arr2.back()`.
2. Add the current element to the array whose last element is greater.
3. After processing all elements, append `arr2` to `arr1`.

This directly follows the problem's distribution rule.

## Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(n)`

## C++ Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> resultArray(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> arr1 = {nums[0]};
        vector<int> arr2 = {nums[1]};

        for (int i = 2; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (arr1.back() > arr2.back())
                arr1.push_back(nums[i]);
            else
                arr2.push_back(nums[i]);
        }

        arr1.insert(arr1.end(), arr2.begin(), arr2.end());
        return arr1;
    }
};
